1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:28,800
This is Doing and Feeling, the long version. Episode 2, Minds and Other Bodies. In 2002, researchers at Oxford published a short paper in Science about a new Caledonian crow named Betty. Betty and a male companion, Abel, were offered a little bucket of food at the bottom of a vertical tube, along with two pieces of wire, one straight and one hooked. Abel flew off with the hook. Betty picked up the straight wire, wedged one end, bent it into a hook, and fished out the bucket.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,820
on later trials. It looked like insight, a bird solving a new problem by making a tool it had

3
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:41,680
never been taught to make. Fourteen years later, a team led by Christian Rutz complicated the story.

4
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:48,200
Some wild-caught New Caledonian crows, kept briefly in field aviaries, bent straight twigs

5
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:54,080
without needing to, and one of them used Betty's exact wedge-and-pull technique. Bending may simply

6
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:59,280
be part of the species' toolkit, so Betty may have been running a routine rather than having a flash

7
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,680
The authors did not rule inside out.

8
00:01:02,980 --> 00:01:06,660
Either way, the story carries the lesson of this whole episode.

9
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:08,180
Here's the argument.

10
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:12,920
Intelligence has evolved again and again in very different bodies and brains,

11
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:18,160
in birds, in octopuses, in insects, and in simpler forms in cells and collectives.

12
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:23,640
It is a family of solutions to the problems of staying alive, not a ladder with us at the top.

13
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:26,440
And when we study it, we err in both directions.

14
00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:30,260
We see minds that are not there, and we miss minds that are.

15
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:32,820
Start with the two errors.

16
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:38,560
In the early 1900s, a horse in Berlin called Clever Hans appeared to do arithmetic,

17
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:40,700
tapping out answers with a hoof.

18
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:48,060
The psychologist Oskar Funxt showed that Hans was reading tiny, involuntary cues in his questioner's posture,

19
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:50,560
which relaxed when he reached the right number.

20
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:53,440
Hans was clever, just not at arithmetic.

21
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:59,240
The corrective principle, called Morgan's Canon after the psychologist Conway Lloyd Morgan,

22
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:04,120
says not to credit a behavior to a higher faculty if a lower one would explain it.

23
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:08,580
Clever hands is the founding parable of machine learning evaluation.

24
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:14,100
Two, a model that scores well by exploiting a spurious cue in its data

25
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:16,960
is literally called a clever hands predictor.

26
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:19,240
And he'll be back when we get to machines.

27
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:24,400
The opposite error is refusing to credit a mind even when the evidence is strong.

28
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:28,680
The primatologist Franz de Waal called it anthropodennial

29
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:31,520
and argued that the history of animal cognition

30
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:37,060
is largely a history of humans testing animals badly and then underestimating them.

31
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:41,560
Part of the problem is what the biologist Jakob van Uckschool called the umwelt,

32
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:43,920
an animal's own perceptual world.

33
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:48,800
A tick waiting on a branch responds to little more than the smell of mammal skin,

34
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:55,480
warmth, and the texture of hair. A test designed around human senses and human interests can

35
00:02:55,480 --> 00:03:01,180
easily miss what another animal is good at. So every result in this episode comes with the same

36
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:08,200
question attached. Which of the two errors is it most at risk of? Begin with the birds. New

37
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:15,320
Caledonian crows do not just use tools. In 2018, a team showed them assembling compound tools,

38
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:19,140
fitting short pieces together into one long enough to reach a reward,

39
00:03:19,380 --> 00:03:20,860
without having been shown how.

40
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:22,500
Ravens appeared a plan.

41
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:29,160
In 2017, Kankabadaya and Mathias Osvath showed that ravens would select a tool,

42
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:31,820
or a token they could later trade for food,

43
00:03:31,980 --> 00:03:35,120
and keep it for use after a delay as long as a night,

44
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:38,740
performing about as well as great apes on similar tasks.

45
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:41,580
And scrub jays seemed to model other minds.

46
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:45,440
In a classic study by Nathan Emery and Nicola Clayton,

47
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:50,220
jays that had been watched while hiding food often came back alone and moved it,

48
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:53,020
but only if they had stolen from others themselves.

49
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:56,480
Experienced thieves seemed to anticipate theft.

50
00:03:56,920 --> 00:04:00,120
How do birds manage this with brains the size of a walnut?

51
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:04,920
Part of the answer came in 2016, when Sueren Olkovich,

52
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:07,920
Susanna Herculano, Uzel, and colleagues

53
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:11,320
counted neurons in the brains of 28 bird species.

54
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:17,980
Parrots and songbirds pack neurons about twice, as densely as primates with brains of the same mass.

55
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:23,500
A large macaws forebrain holds at least as many neurons as a macaque monkey's cortex,

56
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:25,880
in a brain a fraction of the size.

57
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:32,220
Corvids still fall well short of great apes, so the claim is not that crows equal chimpanzees.

58
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:35,980
It's that a small brain can carry a great deal of computing power.

59
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:39,380
Birds also lack the layered cortex that mammals have,

60
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:42,000
which long made their intelligence look like a puzzle.

61
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:46,860
In 2020, Martin Stasho, Honor Gunter-Kuhn, and colleagues

62
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:51,240
found that parts of the bird forebrain contain repeating, column-like circuits

63
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:55,840
strikingly similar to the organization of our cortex, though not in every region.

64
00:04:56,360 --> 00:05:02,640
Then, in February 2025, a set of papers in Science traced how these bird circuits develop.

65
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:05,860
The circuits arise from different developmental origins

66
00:05:05,860 --> 00:05:11,560
and mostly different gene programs than the mammalian cortex, though a few neuron types do

67
00:05:11,560 --> 00:05:17,760
look shared. Birds and mammals last shared an ancestor around 320 million years ago.

68
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,440
What they share is not an inherited cortex. It's largely a convergent solution, arrived at twice.

69
00:05:25,060 --> 00:05:31,060
Biologists treat convergence as nature's own replication study. Eyes evolved many times,

70
00:05:31,060 --> 00:05:37,700
and so did flight. When unrelated lineages arrive at similar answers, it suggests the answer is

71
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:43,900
shaped by the problem more than by the ancestry. That is a claim about organization, not about any

72
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:51,600
particular tissue. The octopus pushes the point further. Octopuses are mollusks, closer to clams

73
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:57,240
and snails than to us, and the last common ancestor of octopuses and humans lived perhaps

74
00:05:57,240 --> 00:06:04,400
600 million years ago, probably a small, simple, worm-like animal. Everything sophisticated about

75
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:10,020
octopus cognition evolved separately from ours. Separately is doing a lot of work there.

76
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:16,100
Octopuses still have neurons, synapses, and many of the same molecular parts we do.

77
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:22,140
True, and it's worth being precise. The building blocks are shared, inherited from that distant

78
00:06:22,140 --> 00:06:27,980
ancestor. Nerve cells, the basic signaling machinery, many of the same genes. When the

79
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:34,180
octopus genome was sequenced in 2015, it even showed a large expansion of a gene family called

80
00:06:34,180 --> 00:06:40,120
the protocaterines, which help wire up neurons, a family that had also expanded, independently,

81
00:06:40,460 --> 00:06:46,000
in vertebrates. What evolved separately is the large-scale architecture. How many neurons,

82
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:51,920
how they're organized, and what they do. Same bricks, different buildings. An octopus has

83
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:57,840
500 million neurons, and about two-thirds of them are in its arms rather than its central brain.

84
00:06:58,120 --> 00:07:04,360
The arms carry out complex actions semi-independently. Octopeuses solve puzzles,

85
00:07:04,740 --> 00:07:09,880
escape from tanks, recognize individual people, and appear to play. Their relatives,

86
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:16,720
cuttlefish, passed a version of the marshmallow test in a 2021 study led by Alexandra Schnell.

87
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:20,880
They would pass up a less preferred food to get a preferred one later,

88
00:07:22,140 --> 00:07:27,300
from under a minute to more than two minutes, and the best waiters were also the fastest learners.

89
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:32,980
There's a puzzle in their life history, too. Most octopuses live only a year or two,

90
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:37,300
and many die soon after breeding, so the young never meet their parents.

91
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:44,320
In our own lineage, big brains go with long lives and long childhoods. A 2019 review by

92
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:49,420
Piro Amodio, Nicola Clayton and colleagues suggested that losing the protective shell

93
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:55,280
drove cephalopod intelligence. Once exposed, these animals needed flexible ways to hunt

94
00:07:55,280 --> 00:08:00,680
and to avoid being eaten. Whatever the right answer turns out to be, it's a different route

95
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:06,520
to a large brain under different pressures, which is exactly what you'd expect if intelligence is a

96
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:12,100
family of solutions rather than a ladder. The philosopher Peter Godfrey Smith, who has spent

97
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:17,740
years diving with octopuses, wrote that meeting one is probably the closest we will come to meeting

98
00:08:19,420 --> 00:08:23,640
We'll keep returning to the octopus in the philosophy of Episode 9,

99
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:28,640
the evidence of Episode 10, and the machine debates of Episodes 12 and 13,

100
00:08:29,180 --> 00:08:33,400
because it shows that complex cognition can be built on a radically different plan.

101
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:37,380
Insects push the size question to an extreme.

102
00:08:37,820 --> 00:08:43,340
A honeybee brain has about a million neurons, nearly 100,000 times fewer than ours.

103
00:08:43,780 --> 00:08:49,280
Yet honeybees perform the waggle dance, the symbolic communication of direction and distance,

104
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:53,720
that won Carl von Frisch a share of the 1973 Nobel Prize.

105
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:56,180
How can so few neurons do so much?

106
00:08:56,340 --> 00:09:04,000
In a 2009 review, Lars Chitka and Jeremy Niven argued that many abilities we associate with big brains

107
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,200
need surprisingly small circuits, and that bigger brains often buy more detail,

108
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:14,700
more memory, and more parallel processing, rather than qualitatively new kinds of thinking.

109
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:17,460
Bumblebees show something closer to culture.

110
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:25,280
Sylvain Allem, Lars Chitka, and colleagues hid an artificial flower under a clear plastic sheet,

111
00:09:25,580 --> 00:09:32,280
reachable only by pulling a string. Left to themselves, just two of 291 bees solved it.

112
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:37,680
But most bees that watched a trained demonstrator learned it, and the skill spread through colonies.

113
00:09:38,180 --> 00:09:42,560
The mechanism matters, though. Observers seem to have been drawn to the string,

114
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:48,060
and then to have learned by trial and error. That is not imitation of the pulling movement,

115
00:09:49,420 --> 00:09:56,580
says to take that reading seriously. In 2024, Chitka's group went further. They built a two-step

116
00:09:56,580 --> 00:10:02,920
puzzle box that no bee solved on its own, over 12 days or more of access, yet several bees learned

117
00:10:02,920 --> 00:10:08,540
it from trained demonstrators. That's social learning of a behavior too complex to invent alone,

118
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:16,560
which in humans we treat as the seed of cumulative culture. And in 2022, the same lab reported that

119
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:22,580
bumblebees rolled small balls over and over with no reward and came to prefer a colored chamber

120
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:28,900
they associated with the balls. Play in an insect. That's exactly where I'd reach for Morgan's canon.

121
00:10:29,220 --> 00:10:35,440
So did the authors by design. They checked the standard criteria animal behaviorists use for

122
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:41,280
play. The behavior is voluntary, not directly rewarded, repeated, and done when the animal

123
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:46,600
is relaxed rather than stressed. Whether anything is enjoyed is a question for part two.

124
00:10:46,940 --> 00:10:54,000
As behavior, it met the criteria. Is there a general intelligence in animals, like human G?

125
00:10:54,540 --> 00:11:00,680
Within species, increasingly yes. Louis Matzel and colleagues found that mice good at one kind

126
00:11:00,680 --> 00:11:05,780
of learning tend to be good at others, with a general factor accounting for roughly two-fifths

127
00:11:05,780 --> 00:11:11,400
of the differences on their test batteries. Across species, Simon Reeder, Ifka Hodger,

128
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:17,460
and Kevin Laland compiled reports of innovation, social learning, and tool use for 62 primate

129
00:11:17,460 --> 00:11:22,760
species, and found that a single dimension captured most of the variation, with high

130
00:11:22,760 --> 00:11:29,460
scores evolving independently in several lineages—capuchins, baboons, macaques, and great apes.

131
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:39,640
In a 2017 paper in the journal Behavioral and Brain Sciences, Judith Burkhart, Michael Schubiger, and Karel van Skijk drew these strands together,

132
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:47,340
arguing that general intelligence is not unique to humans and linking its evolution to how much animals learn from one another.

133
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:52,400
But the measurement problems are serious, and they're the problems of episode one in new clothes.

134
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:56,360
In animal studies, being keen on food can look like being clever.

135
00:11:57,140 --> 00:12:00,800
Samples are often small and drawn from a single laboratory strain,

136
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,100
and species differ in what they're good at.

137
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:08,200
In 2007, Esther Hermann and colleagues gave the same broad battery

138
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:12,240
to two-and-a-half-year-old children, chimpanzees, and orangutans.

139
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:15,940
On physical problems about tools, quantities, and space,

140
00:12:16,160 --> 00:12:18,600
the toddlers and the apes performed similarly.

141
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:23,820
On social problems, such as following someone's gaze or learning by watching,

142
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:25,820
the toddlers were far ahead.

143
00:12:26,360 --> 00:12:28,780
Humans do not sit at the top of a single ladder.

144
00:12:29,080 --> 00:12:32,000
We are unusually specialized for learning from each other.

145
00:12:32,380 --> 00:12:34,280
Brains tell the same mixed story.

146
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:43,220
In 2014, Evan McLean and dozens of collaborators tested self-control in 567 animals from 36 species.

147
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:48,640
Absolute brain size predicted performance better than brain size relative to the body.

148
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:53,160
And among primates, the breadth of a species' diet predicted self-control,

149
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:55,660
while the size of its social groups did not.

150
00:12:56,360 --> 00:12:58,400
Different pressures produce different profiles.

151
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:01,700
Now go below the level of brains.

152
00:13:02,040 --> 00:13:05,920
The biologist Michael Levin studies what he calls basal cognition,

153
00:13:06,340 --> 00:13:08,640
problem-solving by cells and tissues.

154
00:13:08,940 --> 00:13:14,020
His framework, called TAME, borrows an old criterion from William James

155
00:13:14,020 --> 00:13:18,340
that intelligence is the ability to reach the same goal by different means.

156
00:13:18,740 --> 00:13:21,160
Levin's lab studies bioelectricity,

157
00:13:21,380 --> 00:13:26,120
the patterns of voltage across cell membranes that help coordinate how tissues grow.

158
00:13:26,860 --> 00:13:30,380
Flatworms called planaria can regrow a whole body from a fragment.

159
00:13:30,760 --> 00:13:35,340
In one experiment, briefly blocking the electrical connections between cells

160
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:37,640
made some fragments regrow with two heads,

161
00:13:37,780 --> 00:13:42,420
and those worms kept regrowing two heads when cut again, with no further treatment.

162
00:13:42,760 --> 00:13:46,660
Even some treated worms that looked normal grew two heads when they were cut later,

163
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:48,980
as if a stored pattern had been rewritten.

164
00:13:49,820 --> 00:13:55,300
Levin's group has also built xenobots from frog cells and anthrobots from human airway cells,

165
00:13:56,400 --> 00:14:01,820
constructs that move on their own. What they show about cognition, as opposed to the remarkable

166
00:14:01,820 --> 00:14:07,860
plasticity of development, is still argued over. Even a single cell can solve spatial problems.

167
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:14,760
The slime mold Fisarum polycephalum, one giant cell with many nuclei, finds short paths through

168
00:14:14,760 --> 00:14:21,320
mazes. In a 2010 experiment, researchers placed oat flakes in the positions of the cities around

169
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:26,820
Tokyo, and the slime mold grew a network of tubes that closely matched the efficiency and

170
00:14:26,820 --> 00:14:33,080
resilience of the Tokyo rail system. That's a distributed optimizer. I'm not sure I'd call it

171
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:39,400
intelligent. That's the live debate, and it's partly about vocabulary. Levin would say intelligence

172
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:45,160
comes in degrees, how well a system navigates toward goals in some space, whether physical,

173
00:14:45,160 --> 00:14:51,580
anatomical, or behavioral. Critics reply that stretching the word to slime molds empties it.

174
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:57,520
Both sides agree about the phenomena. Plants are where that disagreement gets heated,

175
00:14:57,720 --> 00:15:04,200
and where a failed replication is instructive. A century ago, the Indian physicist Jagadish

176
00:15:04,200 --> 00:15:09,120
Chandra Bose built sensitive instruments to record electrical responses in plants,

177
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:14,600
and argued that they had something like nervous reactions, ideas that were partly ahead of their

178
00:15:15,160 --> 00:15:19,980
and partly overstated. Today we know that plants signal electrically and chemically.

179
00:15:20,260 --> 00:15:26,400
A Venus flytrap effectively counts touches, snapping shut after two and starting digestion

180
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:32,280
after more. The sensitive plant mimosa stops folding its leaves when it is repeatedly dropped

181
00:15:32,280 --> 00:15:39,100
without harm, which looks like habituation. In 2016, Monica Gagliano and colleagues reported

182
00:15:39,100 --> 00:15:43,500
that pea seedlings could learn to associate a breeze with light, a form of conditioning.

183
00:15:45,280 --> 00:15:52,660
a replication by the plant biologist Casey Markle found no evidence of it. The original team argued

184
00:15:52,660 --> 00:15:57,660
that his setup differed in key ways, and he replied that those differences could not explain

185
00:15:57,660 --> 00:16:03,620
the result. And in 2019, Lincoln Tays and colleagues argued that plants neither possess

186
00:16:03,620 --> 00:16:09,840
nor require consciousness. Most plant scientists accept sophisticated signaling and adaptive

187
00:16:09,840 --> 00:16:15,980
behavior. Far fewer accept talk of plant mines. Here the error at risk was over-tribution,

188
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:19,560
and so far the claim has not held up under independent testing.

189
00:16:20,820 --> 00:16:27,180
Finally, mines made of many. A honeybee swarm chooses a new home through a process the biologist

190
00:16:27,180 --> 00:16:33,740
Thomas Seeley described in Honeybee Democracy. Scouts advertise candidate sites by dancing,

191
00:16:34,060 --> 00:16:39,480
support builds, and the swarm commits when a quorum forms, a process that resembles the way

192
00:16:39,840 --> 00:16:42,200
accumulate evidence toward a decision.

193
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:44,740
Human groups may have something similar.

194
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:47,700
Anita Woolley and colleagues have proposed

195
00:16:47,700 --> 00:16:49,920
a collective intelligence factor for groups,

196
00:16:50,060 --> 00:16:54,560
and a 2021 meta-analysis found that how a group collaborates

197
00:16:54,560 --> 00:16:58,120
predicts that factor about twice as well as the skills of its members,

198
00:16:58,540 --> 00:17:01,280
though whether groups really have a general factor at all

199
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:02,660
is still argued over.

200
00:17:03,900 --> 00:17:07,580
Crows, octopuses, bees, slime molds, swarms.

201
00:17:07,860 --> 00:17:09,720
What ties the menagerie together?

202
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:17,300
Organization. Intelligence is multiply realized. It evolved separately in birds, cephalopods,

203
00:17:17,400 --> 00:17:23,040
and insects, on different body plans and brain architectures, and evolution keeps converging

204
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:28,520
on similar solutions. That's one of the strongest arguments that what matters is organization

205
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:33,760
rather than any one kind of tissue, and we'll test that argument hard in parts two and three.

206
00:17:34,220 --> 00:17:35,840
It's a family, not a ladder.

207
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:37,920
Species differ in profile,

208
00:17:37,920 --> 00:17:41,060
and the human specialty seems to be learning from one another.

209
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:43,260
And our methods err both ways.

210
00:17:43,620 --> 00:17:45,820
Clever hands, the doubts about Betty,

211
00:17:46,020 --> 00:17:48,700
and the pea seedlings warn against seeing too much.

212
00:17:49,400 --> 00:17:51,280
Anthropodennial, the neuron counts

213
00:17:51,280 --> 00:17:53,320
and the bees warn against seeing too little.

214
00:17:53,600 --> 00:17:56,600
The same tension will haunt every claim about machines.

